Update 2026-09-07 (trust model, WeirdIKE 51c6dcb): the EAP server certificate gets a TRUST MODEL
instead of the single mandatory ca_pem. cfg.trust_mode (weirdike_trust_mode_t): ANCHOR_PEM (0,
today's behaviour: own anchors in ca_pem, mandatory), HOST_STORE (public CAs of the host trust
store), HOST_STORE_PLUS_PEM (host store + extras), NONE (no chain/identity check -- the IKE_AUTH
signature is STILL verified, and the IKE IDr policy remote_id still applies). Exactly TWO PEM
blocks: ca_pem = trust anchors (need not be self-signed; an intermediate listed here ends the
chain; the exact or self-signed server certificate may be an anchor -> adapter pins a leaf that is
byte-identical to an anchor), extra_pem = chain material only (never ends a chain). The host trust
store is a HOST hook (weirdike_crypto_mbedtls_set_host_store; WeirdOS: ESP-IDF certificate bundle
via ipsec_trust_store.cpp); missing store -> the core REFUSES the host-store modes at start, no
fallback. CI: x509 selftest per mode + strongSwan 3-tier PKI matrix (14 cases: NONE ok / IDr still
enforced, host store ok / foreign / absent, host+pem extra anchor, intermediate as chain material
vs as explicit anchor, self-signed server cert as own anchor, exact-leaf pin ok / wrong leaf).
Migration in WeirdOS: existing profiles with ca-pem keep "own anchor" semantics; new EAP profiles
default to "public CAs". Hardware gate with real EAP credentials: PENDING (user-run).

Update 2026-09-07 (LANCOM diagnosis, WeirdIKE be14466): IKE_AUTH outcome semantically separated in
the diag -- ike_auth_rejected (peer error notify, NO AUTH payload from it = it rejected OUR
authentication), auth_local_fail (peer AUTH present, our verification failed), child_notify ONLY
for a Child SA rejected after a successful IKE_AUTH; weirdike_notify_name(); secrets-free wire
summaries "IKE_AUTH #1 wire: auth/IDi/IDr/AUTH/CP/SAi2/TSi/TSr" before sending and "IKE_AUTH
response payloads: IDr/AUTH/CP/SA/TSi/TSr/EAP/NOTIFY" after decrypt. IKE Config Mode for PSK:
CP(CFG_REQUEST) now also in the PSK IKE_AUTH when request_cp is set (RFC 7296 2.19; previously
only the EAP round carried it -- the PSK path silently ignored request_cp). CI: wrong-PSK case
asserts the semantic split; strongSwan PSK + pool (m1-cp.conf) proves the virtual IP via CP.
NOT claimed: that the missing CP caused LANCOM's AUTHENTICATION_FAILED (24) -- see the report.

